"""Render the static interim page from computed dataset summaries."""
import json,pathlib
P=pathlib.Path('data/processed');s=json.loads((P/'summary.json').read_text());f=json.loads((P/'findings.json').read_text())
# Narrative is reviewed for this frozen milestone snapshot; fail rather than publish stale prose.
assert (s['candidate_count'], s['eligible_trials'], s['usable_trials'], f['landscape_trials'], f['pairs'], f['reason_total']) == (335,201,82,80,38,2510), 'New snapshot: review narrative/counts before rebuilding the interim page'
page=f'''<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Who Leaves Clinical Trials? · STATS401 Interim Check-In</title><meta name="description" content="Three static views of participant attrition in randomized depression trials, with reproducible registry data and evaluation plans."><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"></head>
<body><main><header><p class="eyebrow">STATS401 · Interim check-in · Alicia Lu &amp; Vera Piao</p><h1>Who Leaves Clinical Trials?</h1><p class="subtitle">Visualizing Participant Attrition in Randomized Depression Studies</p><p>Randomization makes trial arms comparable at baseline, but some participants do not finish. We examine how reported non-completion varies across depression trials, differs between arms, and is explained in registry records. These views are for clinical researchers, biostatistics students, and readers assessing retention and reporting.</p><nav aria-label="Page sections"><a href="#dataset">Dataset</a> · <a href="#visualizations">Three visualizations</a> · <a href="#evaluation">Evaluation plan</a> · <a href="#limitations">Limitations</a> · <a href="https://github.com/AliciaLuGuyun/STATS401---Clinical-Trial-Attrition">Code &amp; documentation</a></nav></header>
<section id="dataset"><h2>Dataset</h2><h3>Raw data</h3><p>Source: <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/">ClinicalTrials.gov</a>, retrieved September 13, 2026 through the <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/data-api/api">v2 REST API</a>. ClinicalTrials.gov’s source processing date is <strong>{', '.join(s['source_processing_dates'])}</strong> (API versionHolder). Four paginated JSON responses contain <strong>{s['candidate_count']} candidate studies</strong> (about 24 MB). The search requests major depressive disorder, completed interventional Phase II/III studies with results. Search expansion also returns related diagnoses, so the search itself is not the final cohort.</p><p>Source fields include conditions, allocation, intervention model, phase, sponsor, completion date, protocol arm labels/types, and participant-flow periods, groups, STARTED, COMPLETED and non-completion reasons. The full timestamped responses remain local and unchanged; their URLs and SHA-256 checksums are in the <a href="data/processed/source_manifest.json">source manifest</a>.</p>
<h3>Processed data</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Snapshot tables; rows represent aggregate records, not individual patient data</caption><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Rows</th><th>Unit and content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="data/processed/candidates.csv">Candidates</a></td><td>335</td><td>One NCT ID; inclusion/exclusion flags</td></tr><tr><td><a href="data/processed/trials.csv">Trials</a></td><td>{s['eligible_trials']}</td><td>One eligible NCT ID; design, reporting/QC and derived attrition</td></tr><tr><td><a href="data/processed/arms.csv">Arms</a></td><td>{s['single_period_arm_rows']}</td><td>NCT ID + flow group + period; single-period trials, including flagged mappings</td></tr><tr><td><a href="data/processed/reasons.csv">Reasons</a></td><td>{s['single_period_reason_rows']}</td><td>Arm-period + original reason/entry index; labels and reported counts</td></tr><tr><td><a href="data/processed/pairs.csv">Arm pairs</a></td><td>{s['paired_trials']}</td><td>One unambiguously paired two-arm trial</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>Cleaning and processing completed</h3><p>335 candidates → 270 randomized → 252 parallel-group → 211 with explicit major-depression condition labels → <strong>201 after deferring 10 mixed bipolar/schizophrenia records</strong>. Broader or mixed diagnostic records require review; this conservative interim subset is not an exhaustive depression-trial census.</p><p>51 trials have multiple periods and 106 need arm-mapping review (these flags overlap). We do not select an ambiguous period or infer a treatment label. Exact arm-label matching, allowing only case and whitespace normalization, plus a single reported period and valid counts yields <strong>{s['usable_trials']} usable trials / {s['usable_arms']} arms</strong>. The landscape omits two trials without completion dates. See the <a href="docs/DATA_DICTIONARY.md">data dictionary and rules</a> and <a href="data/processed/validation.json">validation results</a>.</p><p class="definition"><strong>Attrition here = 1 − COMPLETED / STARTED</strong> in the sole reported participant-flow period. STARTED must be positive and COMPLETED must lie between zero and STARTED. Trial totals sum across matched arms before dividing. Missing counts remain missing. Enrollment is never the denominator. “Overall Study” can include follow-up; this is reported-period non-completion, not a standardized treatment-discontinuation rate.</p></section>
<section id="visualizations"><h2>Interim visualizations</h2><p>All three figures are generated from the processed snapshot. They are static for this milestone; the interaction plans below describe later work.</p>
<article id="landscape"><h3>1. Attrition across trials</h3><p><strong>Question:</strong> How much does reported non-completion vary across eligible trials?</p><figure><a href="figures/01_attrition_landscape.svg" aria-label="Open attrition landscape at full size"><img src="figures/01_attrition_landscape.svg" alt="Scatterplot of 80 trials by completion year and non-completion percentage, with bubble area showing participants started. Median 13.1 percent; rates range from zero to 100 percent."></a><figcaption>80 trials, completion years {f['completion_year_min']}–{f['completion_year_max']}; each bubble is one trial. <a href="data/processed/plot_landscape.csv">Plot data</a> · Click figure for full size.</figcaption></figure><p>The median trial has <strong>{f['median_trial_attrition']:.1%} non-completion</strong>; rates range from 0% to 100%. The 100% record (<a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00149643?tab=results">NCT00149643</a>) explicitly reports zero completions in both arms and no reason table. It is retained as reported, not treated as a verified real-world dropout finding. Completion year provides context, not a common follow-up duration or evidence of a time trend.</p><p><strong>Design:</strong> points; horizontal position = year, vertical position = rate, area = STARTED. Position supports rate comparison; area gives approximate scale. One color avoids suggesting an untested subgroup relationship. Rates use the full 0–100% scale.</p><p class="plan"><strong>Interaction plan &amp; purpose:</strong> select a trial to inspect its counts, period and NCT record; filter by phase/sponsor/year; highlight that trial in the other views. This will connect overall patterns to the reporting context of individual trials.</p></article>
<article id="differences"><h3>2. Within-trial attrition differences</h3><p><strong>Question:</strong> Which arm has higher non-completion within each defensibly paired trial?</p><figure><a href="figures/02_arm_differences.svg" aria-label="Open arm differences at full size"><img src="figures/02_arm_differences.svg" alt="Diverging dot plot of all 38 paired trials, sorted by experimental minus comparator attrition. Differences range from minus 20 to plus 25 percentage points."></a><figcaption>All 38 paired trials, sorted from negative to positive down the left panel, then down the right. <a href="data/processed/plot_pairs.csv">Arm labels, counts and plot data</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>Experimental-arm non-completion is higher in <strong>{f['experimental_higher']}</strong> trials, comparator-arm non-completion is higher in <strong>{f['comparator_higher']}</strong>, and <strong>{f['equal']}</strong> are equal. Differences range from {f['pair_difference_min_pp']:.0f} to +{f['pair_difference_max_pp']:.0f} percentage points. Comparators vary; these descriptive differences do not estimate a pooled treatment effect or statistical significance.</p><p><strong>Design:</strong> dots positioned on a shared, symmetric percentage-point scale, with a zero reference. Blue/orange reinforces direction already shown by position. Each NCT ID remains labeled; no subset of extreme trials is selected.</p><p class="plan"><strong>Interaction plan &amp; purpose:</strong> switch sorting between signed and absolute difference, select a trial to show both STARTED/COMPLETED fractions, and link selection to the landscape. This will help viewers inspect imbalance without losing denominators or comparator identity.</p></article>
<article id="reasons"><h3>3. Reported reasons for non-completion</h3><p><strong>Question:</strong> Which original registry labels account for the largest reported counts?</p><figure><a href="figures/03_reported_reasons.svg" aria-label="Open reason chart at full size"><img src="figures/03_reported_reasons.svg" alt="Ranked horizontal bars showing the ten most frequent original reason labels. Adverse Event leads with 569, followed by Withdrawal by Subject with 465 and Lost to Follow-up with 380."></a><figcaption>{f['reason_arms']} reconciled arms from {f['reason_trials']} trials; {f['reason_total']:,} reported non-completions. Top ten labels account for {f['top10_total']:,}; <a href="data/processed/plot_reasons_all.csv">all {f['reason_label_count']} labels and counts</a> are available.</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Adverse Event ({f['top_reason_count']})</strong> is the largest individual label. “Withdrawal by Subject” and “Withdrawal of Consent” remain separate: no synonym merging has been applied. Only arms whose reported reasons sum to STARTED − COMPLETED contribute. Another 69 otherwise usable arms lack a reason sum; they contribute no assumed zeroes.</p><p><strong>Design:</strong> bars sorted by count; length and a zero-based horizontal position encode reported participants. Original labels are retained, including wording differences. Larger trials have more weight, and missing reporting limits generalization. Counts are neither incidence rates nor harmonized categories.</p><p class="plan"><strong>Interaction plan &amp; purpose:</strong> select a label to reveal contributing trials and original wording; filter by selected trial/arm; toggle counts and within-arm proportions with explicit denominators. This will separate sample-size effects from the composition of reported departures.</p></article></section>
<section id="evaluation"><h2>Evaluation plan</h2><h3>What we will evaluate</h3><p>Can viewers identify high-attrition trials, interpret the sign of an arm difference, and find a common reported reason? Are labels, bubble sizes and denominators clear? Does the sequence explain the findings and incomplete-reporting caveat? Once interactions exist, do filters and linked selection help these tasks?</p><h3>How we will evaluate it</h3><p>Invite 4–6 classmates for a brief, informal usability session. First test the static page; repeat the interaction tasks after implementation. Give five tasks: locate a high-attrition trial; identify which arm has higher attrition for a specified NCT ID; identify the largest original reason label; explain why missing reasons are not zero; summarize the three-view story. Later, add a phase-filter and linked-selection task. Use answers checked against the CSVs, observe completion and ask participants to explain confusing points.</p><h3>Feedback/data we will collect</h3><p>Record task correctness and completion, approximate time, assistance needed, confusion or interaction errors, 1–5 clarity/usefulness ratings and short written comments. Use anonymous participant codes; do not collect health information. Summarize recurring issues and prioritize changes. This evaluation is planned; no user feedback has yet been collected.</p></section>
<section id="limitations"><h2>Current limitations</h2><ul><li>Results-bearing, completed registry studies are a selected subset; strict label matching further narrows coverage. Multi-period and unresolved mappings remain in the QC tables for review.</li><li>Condition labels do not guarantee a homogeneous major-depression population. Comorbidities, treatments, age groups, comparators and follow-up differ.</li><li>The sole registered period is not necessarily treatment-only. These figures describe period non-completion and cannot identify individual predictors, causes, or treatment-effect bias.</li><li>Original reason labels are heterogeneous; the top-ten chart does not merge synonyms. Registry entries may contain reporting errors, even when the arithmetic is valid.</li><li>Raw data are a local snapshot. Registry updates can change a new download; committed processed tables and manifest preserve what these figures used.</li></ul><h2>Next steps</h2><p>Review flagged periods and arm links with the team, validate unusual records, agree on a conservative reason taxonomy, then add the planned D3 interactions and test them with classmates. The final five-view system, report and poster are later milestones.</p></section>
<footer><p>Reproduce this page and its figures using the <a href="https://github.com/AliciaLuGuyun/STATS401---Clinical-Trial-Attrition#readme">repository instructions</a>. <a href="docs/INTERIM_AUDIT.md">Milestone audit</a> · <a href="docs/VISUALIZATION_DESIGN.md">Design and evaluation details</a></p><p>Definitions: <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/policy/results-definitions">ClinicalTrials.gov results data elements</a>. No individual patient records are used.</p></footer></main></body></html>'''
pathlib.Path('index.html').write_text(page)
